\description{
These functions wrap \code{{cli}} functions and elements
to build an attractive command line interface (CLI).
They divide into those that change what a function does
and those that only report what it did.

A call that changes control flow always fires,
whatever the verbosity, because silencing it would let the code it guards
run on and return a wrong answer instead of an explanation:
\itemize{
\item \code{snet_abort()} for an error the user has to fix.
\item \code{snet_unavailable()} for a feature that is not yet available.
\item \code{snet_warn()} for a result the user should not trust without reading,
such as a value that is dropped or a name that does not match.
\item \code{snet_deprecated()} for an old name of a function, argument, or value.
An old name that is only superseded is reported as \code{snet_minor_info()}.
\item \code{snet_moved()} for a function that moved to another package.
\item \code{snet_requires()} for a package that a function needs and that is not
installed.
}

A call that only reports is silenced under the default verbosity:
\itemize{
\item \code{snet_info()} for what a function chose on the user's behalf.
\item \code{snet_success()} for the completion of a long task.
\item \code{snet_progress_step()} and the other \verb{snet_progress_*()} functions
for a progress bar.
\item \code{snet_minor_info()} for the bookkeeping a function did on the way,
such as which column it renamed.
}

\code{snet_prompt()} asks the user something, so it always shows.
}
\details{
\code{snet_moved()} tells the user that a function moved to another package,
and how to install that package where it is missing.
By default it then aborts, since the old name is defunct.
With \code{stage = "deprecated"} it warns instead, so that a shim can go on to
call the function in its new package.

\code{snet_requires()} checks that a package is installed before a function
uses it, and aborts with the command to install it where it is not.
}
\section{Verbosity}{

The \code{snet_verbosity} option takes four levels, each adding one thing to
the one before it:
\itemize{
\item \code{'quiet'}, the default, reports nothing that is not an error,
a warning, or a prompt.
\item \code{'normal'} adds \code{snet_info()} and \code{snet_success()},
so that a user learns what a function chose on their behalf.
\item \code{'verbose'} adds the progress bars,
which reassure a user that a long task is running.
\item \code{'debug'} adds \code{snet_minor_info()},
which reports exhaustively what a function did on the way.
}

Set one with, for example, \code{options(snet_verbosity = 'verbose')}.
}

